  "body": "Closes #97\nClaim: ext/bybifok-opus-1\nAuthor model: Opus 5.5\n\n**Problem.** LOT-062 kept other clients' numbers byte for byte with a workaround. Every number token was rewritten into a string `\"<U+E000 U+E001><lexeme>\"` before `JSONDecoder`, then unboxed after `JSONEncoder` (`JSONNumberBox`). A real string starting with those two characters came back as a number.\n\n**Cause.** `JSONDecoder` and `JSONEncoder` only know `Int64` and `Double`.\n\n**Change** (spec `docs/lots/LOT-073-mpstorage-json-tokenizer.md`). All new types are internal to HFRModels/MPStorage:\n- **`JSONTokenizer`** reads the text into a `JSONValue` and keeps every number's lexeme. Its grammar is Foundation's `JSONDecoder`'s, checked case by case against it:\n  - RFC 8259, plus a leading byte order mark and trailing commas;\n  - the first duplicate key is kept;\n  - 512 levels deep are read, 513 refused;\n  - an unpaired surrogate still throws `unpairedSurrogate(byteOffset:)`.\n- **`JSONTreeDecoder` and `JSONTreeEncoder`** convert the models as `JSONDecoder` and `JSONEncoder` do (`1.0` and `1e2` decode as `Int`, a non-finite double is refused). A `JSONValue` is read and written whole, so the unmodelled parts keep their lexemes.\n- **`JSONWriter`** writes `JSONEncoder`'s exact bytes (`.sortedKeys`, `.withoutEscapingSlashes`: UTF-8 key order, the same escapes), and numbers as their lexeme.\n- **`MPStorageCodec`** uses them. The 64 KiB cap, the non-BMP guard and its path, and `noWritableEntry` are unchanged. The lenient readers read the number's lexeme with the rules the boxed path had.\n- **Removed**: `JSONNumberBox` and its private-use marker. **Public API** of `JSONValue`, `JSONNumber` and `MPStorageCodec`: unchanged.\n- **Tests**: every existing MPStorage test is unchanged and green. `R60a`'s document is written back byte for byte as `JSONEncoder` wrote it. The writer equals `JSONEncoder` on every control character, on tricky keys and on a nested document. 39 grammar cases and 170 conversions agree with `JSONDecoder`. The old-marker string stays a string, which fails with LOT-062's boxing. 15 mutations are each caught.\n- No CHANGELOG-LEGACY row (no legacy behaviour changes). HFR-PROTOCOL §6c `### LOT-073`. The LOT-062 spec's boxing sentence is updated.\n\n`JSONValue` is a public HFRModels type whose decoding path changes (not its API), so besides the Fable review this needs a review by another contributor.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
